# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: runner-availability-gating — auto-start native (macOS/Windows) jobs only when their runner is online
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN (a CI/CD variable pjb must add)
#+BRANCH: feature-runner-availability-gating

* Context

The macOS/Windows CI runners are intermittent laptops. Native jobs
therefore can't simply be `when: on_success`: when the runner is offline
they pile up as stuck-pending jobs and (if not allow_failure) redden the
pipeline. Until now the manual jobs (vendor:probes, verify:*, pathname:*,
benchmark, release) never auto-ran even when the runner WAS online, and
the test:* jobs auto-ran on master but piled up when the runner was down.

pjb's request: two "is-the-runner-up?" probes, from which the native jobs
depend so they launch AUTOMATICALLY when the runner is online, and fall
back to MANUAL when it is offline (manual trigger always available).

* Problem / GitLab constraint

`rules:` are evaluated at pipeline-CREATION time and cannot see a probe
job's runtime result in the same pipeline. So "on_success if online else
manual" via rules needs the runner status known at creation — impossible
for a same-pipeline probe. Two viable shapes:
  - a parent→child pipeline (detect job passes MACOS/WINDOWS=online var to
    a child whose native jobs use rules on it) — clean, but moves every
    native job into a child YAML (big refactor; breaks parent↔child
    needs:/artifacts);
  - an ORCHESTRATOR that keeps every native job `when: manual` (so offline
    == the manual fallback) and, from the always-on Linux runner, `play`s
    the manual jobs whose runner is online.

* Implemented (orchestrator, chosen for zero disruption)

- scripts/ci-autostart-native-runners.py + the `.pre` job
  `autostart:native-runners` in .gitlab-ci.yml. It queries
  GET /projects/:id/runners?tag_list={macos,windows} for an online runner,
  then `play`s this pipeline's manual jobs carrying that tag. Excludes
  release:* / benchmark:*. FAIL-SAFE: no token / API error => plays
  nothing => every native job stays manual (today's behaviour). Validated:
  the runners API filters by tag and reports online/offline correctly;
  glab ci lint is green.

* To activate (pjb)

1. Add a CI/CD variable RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN = a project access token with
   `api` scope (playing a job is a write; read_api is not enough). Masked;
   protected if auto-start should only happen on protected refs (note: an
   MR pipeline from a non-protected branch then won't get the token, so its
   native jobs stay manual — acceptable, or make it unprotected).
2. Watch a pipeline: online runners => their native manual jobs auto-start;
   offline => they stay manual.

* Follow-ups

- Phase 2 (optional): flip test:* (.platform-test-common) and the encoding
  jobs from `on_success` to `when: manual` so they too fall back to manual
  (no stuck-pending) when the runner is offline, and are auto-started by
  this orchestrator when online. Deferred until the token is confirmed, so
  master test coverage is not left manual-without-auto in the interim.
- Tune SKIP_PREFIXES (or add a per-job marker) if some native jobs should
  NOT auto-run on every push (e.g. the AutoCAD probes while unlicensed).

* Acceptance criteria

- With RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN set: native manual jobs whose runner is online
  start automatically; offline ones stay manual; pipeline never reddens or
  piles up because of an offline laptop.
- Without the token: identical to today (all native jobs manual).

* UPDATE 2026-08-07 — orchestrator-play abandoned; child pipeline adopted

The orchestrator-play approach (v1) was tried on master (pipeline
2740098321) with the token set. The probe CORRECTLY reported
`online native runners: ['macos', 'windows']`, but it played 0 jobs.
Root cause (empirical): a GitLab `when: manual` job only reaches the
`manual` (playable) status once its STAGE is active and its `needs:` are
satisfied — until then it is `created`, and `POST .../jobs/:id/play`
cannot start it. At `.pre` (and even a minute later) the native jobs were
`created`, not `manual` (only build:alfe:* had become manual). The
pipeline jobs API also does not expose each job's `when:`, so
"will-be-manual" jobs can't be pre-identified. A single early play-pass
therefore cannot start a cascade of jobs that only become manual later.
=> orchestrator-play is structurally unable to do the job; removed
(scripts/ci-autostart-native-runners.py deleted).

CHOSEN (pjb, 2026-08-07): the child-pipeline design. Parent
detect:runners (scripts/ci-detect-runners.py) writes a dotenv
MACOS_RUNNER/WINDOWS_RUNNER=online|offline; native:pipeline triggers the
child .gitlab/native.yml passing those vars; the child's native jobs use
rules: evaluated at the CHILD's creation (vars now known) => on_success
when online, `manual` when offline. No `strategy: depend` (a manual-only
child must not hang the parent). Still fail-safe (no token => offline =>
manual). First jobs migrated: conformance:pathname-mapping:{macos,windows}
(self-contained, no needs) as the end-to-end proof; the rest of the native
jobs (test:*, verify:*, vendor:probes:* + build:alfe:* deps, pathname:*,
encoding:*, benchmark:*, release:*) migrate incrementally, each validated
on a real pipeline. Release-tag pipelines: native jobs are on_success
(mandatory) via the `$CI_COMMIT_TAG` rule; a `strategy: depend` may be
wanted on the trigger for tag pipelines so the release waits for them
(follow-up).


Statut 2026-08-10 — implémenté, en attente d'une variable CI/CD
--------------------------------------------------------------

Le mécanisme est en place et vérifié sur master : le job `.pre`
`detect:runners` (scripts/ci-detect-runners.py) publie MACOS_RUNNER /
WINDOWS_RUNNER via un artefact dotenv, `native:pipeline` déclenche le
pipeline enfant .gitlab/native.yml, et les gates `.gate-{macos,windows}-{hard,soft}`
y basculent en `when: on_success` dès que la variable vaut "online",
avec repli `when: manual` sinon (et `allow_failure: false` sur un tag
release pour les gates `hard`).

Il ne reste donc aucun travail de dépôt : le fail-safe fait que sans
RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN (portée api) tout reste `manual`, c'est-à-dire le
comportement d'avant — jamais un faux "online". Le statut passe de TODO
à BLOCKED sur cette variable, que pjb doit ajouter dans les CI/CD
settings du projet.


* DONE 2026-08-20 — la variable a été ajoutée, et le mécanisme est OBSERVÉ

Le ticket était BLOCKED en attente de =RUNNER_STATUS_TOKEN=. La variable
existe désormais, et le comportement n'est plus déduit du code : il est
mesuré sur une pipeline réelle, la 2777241866 de master (2026-08-20
18:58 UTC), qui a eu la bonne fortune de tomber sur un runner en ligne
et un runner hors ligne EN MÊME TEMPS — c'est-à-dire exactement le cas
que les critères d'acceptation demandent de distinguer.

=detect:runners= a publié :

: MACOS_RUNNER=online
: WINDOWS_RUNNER=offline

et dans le pipeline enfant, sans intervention :

| runner  | état    | jobs natifs                                        |
|---------+---------+----------------------------------------------------|
| macOS   | online  | =pending= — démarrés tout seuls (build:alfe:macos, test:*:macos, harvest:sysvars, vendor:probes, pathname:probe, path-sysvars:probe, encoding:experiment, verify:vl-registry, conformance:pathname-mapping) |
| Windows | offline | =manual= — repli, aucun job coincé en pending       |

Les trois critères d'acceptation sont donc satisfaits, et chacun par une
observation plutôt que par lecture du YAML :

- les jobs manuels dont le runner est en ligne démarrent seuls : OUI,
  les macOS sont partis sans que personne ne clique ;
- ceux dont le runner est hors ligne restent manuels : OUI, les trois
  sondes Windows ;
- la pipeline ne rougit pas et ne s'empile pas à cause d'un portable
  éteint : OUI — aucun =stuck_pending= dans cette pipeline.

** Ce que la journée a appris en plus, et qui n'est pas dans les critères

Le repli =manual= ne protège que les pipelines créées PENDANT que le
runner est hors ligne. Un runner qui s'éteint PENDANT un job donne autre
chose : dans la pipeline précédente (2776267840, 13:17), Windows était
en ligne à la création, =path-sysvars:probe:bricscad:windows= a donc
démarré, puis la machine a disparu — le job est mort en
=server_timeout_running= au bout de 1800 s exactement, avec une trace
VIDE, et les deux sondes AutoCAD qui attendaient le runner à
concurrence 1 sont mortes en =stuck_pending_no_matching_runners=.

Ce n'est pas un défaut de la porte : à la création, la décision prise
était juste. Mais cela veut dire qu'un portable qui s'éteint au mauvais
moment coûte encore trois jobs et une demi-heure de runner exclusif, et
c'est =cad-runner-wedged-by-modal-dialog= qui porte ce sujet, pas
celui-ci.
